No project file present, so I'll give you the general recommendation:

### Use `TimeProvider` (built into .NET 8+)

It's the lightest option — no new interfaces, no DI container needed.

**1. Accept `TimeProvider` with a default:**

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**2. In tests, use the built-in fake:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing; // from Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing

var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-30)); // move time back
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` is in the BCL — zero production dependencies
- The optional parameter means existing callers don't break
- One test NuGet: `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`

If you're on **.NET 6/7**, define a tiny `IClock` interface with a single `GetUtcNow()` method instead — same pattern, just not built-in.

